<h2>Why there is no construct &times; tool grid</h2>

<p>
  A cold single-file check times the <strong>whole process</strong>: start the tool, load the standard-library
  stubs, analyze the file. For the heavier tools that fixed startup cost dominates &mdash; their time
  barely moves whichever construct is in the file &mdash; so a fixture-by-fixture grid would mostly
  repeat each tool&rsquo;s baseline {{ benchmarks.rows.length }} times while implying
  &ldquo;tool&nbsp;X takes Y&nbsp;ms on construct&nbsp;Z&rdquo; precision that the measurement cannot
  support. Checkers also don&rsquo;t share a rule taxonomy, so no per-rule cross-tool comparison is
  possible even in principle. We publish what the data actually supports: per-tool distributions above,
  and the genuine departures below. The full per-fixture numbers &mdash; every timing and every
  diagnostic count &mdash; are in the committed CSV if you want them.
</p>

