<p>
  The table above is cold. This one is its opposite: each tool at its
  <strong>fastest achievable repeat-check</strong> of an unchanged file (median across the corpus).
  Only two tools here keep a real cross-run result cache, so only two have a genuinely faster warm
  number &mdash; <strong>Basilisk</strong> (its <code>--cache</code> result-cache) and
  <strong>mypy</strong> (its incremental <code>.mypy_cache</code>).
</p>

<p>
  Pyright, ty and Pyrefly keep <strong>no</strong> cross-run result cache &mdash; they write no
  cache artifacts, expose no cache flag, and a repeat run is no faster than the first beyond
  process/OS warm-up &mdash; so their cold median <em>is</em> their best case. zuban&rsquo;s mypy
  mode <em>does</em> reuse a <code>.mypy_cache</code> when one is present, but the harness
  deliberately wipes it before every timed run to keep the cold table honest, and we publish no
  warm zuban figure because none is measured &mdash; its row shows its cold median, labelled as
  such rather than a fabricated warm number.
</p>

