<h2>Methodology</h2>

<p>
  Every number is produced by <a href="https://github.com/sharkdp/hyperfine">hyperfine</a>, which runs
  each tool&rsquo;s real command-line checker many times and reports the mean wall-clock time. We run
  the <em>same</em> harness against every tool on <em>identical</em> fixtures, so the comparison is
  like-for-like.
</p>

<h3>A crash is never a timing</h3>

<p>
  Fixtures intentionally contain type errors, so tools exit non-zero by design &mdash; which means the
  harness must ignore exit codes while timing. To stop that from recording a <em>crash</em> as a
  fast run, a preflight pass first runs each tool&rsquo;s cold command on every fixture un-timed: a
  tool that fails to analyze a file (exit &ge; 2 &mdash; a parse abort or internal error) is excluded
  from that fixture&rsquo;s timing, together with its warm variant, and its CSV cell stays blank. The
  same pass counts the error diagnostics each tool reports per fixture and writes them to the CSV
  (<code>&lt;tool&gt;_diags</code> columns) &mdash; that is what the <em>Reports diagnostics on</em>
  column summarises, so a run that analyzed a file but flagged nothing is visible in the published
  data, not hidden behind a fast time.
</p>

<h3>Cold, not warm</h3>

<p>
  The main table is a <strong>cold</strong> full-file check from scratch &mdash; the time to start
  the tool and check the file with no prior state. That is the honest worst case, not the warm
  incremental latency you feel while editing. Basilisk and mypy are the only two tools here that keep
  a real cross-run cache (Basilisk a <code>--cache</code> result-cache, mypy an incremental
  <code>.mypy_cache</code>), so their warm re-check is faster again &mdash; that is what the
  <a href="#warm-recheck">warm re-check table</a> shows; mypy&rsquo;s cold number is therefore
  measured under <code>--no-incremental</code>. Pyright, ty and Pyrefly keep no cross-run result cache
  &mdash; a repeat run is a cold run &mdash; and zuban&rsquo;s mypy mode reuses a
  <code>./.mypy_cache</code> when present, so we delete that cache before each timed run. Every number
  in the main table is honestly cold.
</p>

<h3>Fair strictness</h3>

<p>
  mypy runs as <code>mypy --strict --no-incremental --no-error-summary</code> and zuban as
  <code>zuban mypy --strict --no-error-summary</code>, because the fixtures exercise strict-mode
  analysis &mdash; without <code>--strict</code> those tools report &ldquo;no issues&rdquo; on the
  strictness fixtures and would not be doing comparable work. No other flags: in particular no
  <code>--ignore-missing-imports</code>, so failed-import analysis stays part of every tool&rsquo;s
  workload. Basilisk, Pyright, ty and Pyrefly run in their default checking mode. The fixtures are
  plain typing-spec Python with no checker-specific directives, so every tool analyzes the identical
  workload.
</p>

<h3>What this does and doesn&rsquo;t show</h3>

<p>
  These are <strong>cold, full-file CLI runs</strong>. They reflect batch/CI checking of single files,
  not warm incremental editor latency: inside the LSP, an edit re-checks only the file you touched and
  its importers, so interactive re-checks do strictly less work than any number here. Throughput on a
  single fixture is also not the same as throughput on a large real codebase. And because checkers
  implement different analyses (the <em>Reports diagnostics on</em> column makes this visible), a
  faster time can partly reflect a tool doing less &mdash; the diagnostic counts in the CSV let you
  weigh that yourself.
</p>

